# The fixture filesystem df's and quot's b6sim cases report on -- task C4a.
#
# NOT AN IMAGE ANYTHING BOOTS.  It exists because df(1) and quot(1) read a FILESYSTEM, and
# a flat b6fsutil image is an ordinary host file, so those two are the first programs here
# that can be tested under b6sim against real on-disk structure rather than only for their
# argument handling.  ../../ls/README.md's limitation -- b6sim cannot read a directory --
# is what keeps du(1) out of that world; neither of these reads one.
#
# ../../../root.manifest is the real one and this is deliberately nothing like it: no
# device nodes (b6fsutil needs no kernel to make them, but nothing here looks at one), no
# programs, and 200 blocks rather than 2000.  What it has instead is FILE SIZES CHOSEN FOR
# THE ARITHMETIC: every number both programs print is a ceil-divide by the 3072-byte
# filesystem block, multiplied by KBPB into the 1024-byte block they REPORT in
# (../../README.md SS4).  The sizes probe the divide; the multiply is why the third column
# below is always three times the second.
#
#                      bytes   blocks   reported
#   /small                64        1          3   well inside one block
#   /mid                3072        1          3   EXACTLY one block: the off-by-one
#   /big                9216        3          9   three blocks, and DELIBERATELY still
#                                                  direct -- NADDR is 8 with six direct,
#                                                  and an indirect block is one quot does
#                                                  not count, di_size being what it
#                                                  divides.  That gap is icheck(1M)'s
#                                                  question, task C4e, and this fixture
#                                                  does not prejudge it -- ../../icheck/test
#                                                  builds a 600-block one of its own with a
#                                                  519-block file, which is the smallest
#                                                  thing that reaches a DOUBLE indirect
#   /other              1536        1          3   half a block, and so a whole one
#   /d/nested            128        1          3   and a directory to reach it through
#
# /mid IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS.  It is exactly one block, so a ceil-divide that was off by
# one either way moves it to 0 or 2 and the histogram changes shape.  Note it reports 3,
# the same as /small's 64 bytes: a reported block is smaller than an allocated one, so the
# smallest non-empty file still costs three.
#
# THE THREE OWNERS ARE THE POINT OF THE quot CASES.  0 is root, and 201 and 202 are in no
# password file at all, so quot prints the name for the first and its `#%d' fallback for the
# other two -- both arms of that decision in one report.  It used to be the only shape that
# could be diffed anywhere, getpwent(3) having read THE BUILD MACHINE's file under b6sim;
# b6sim serves the target's /etc/passwd now (cmd/sim/etcfiles.cpp) and the fixture keeps the
# three owners because the two arms are still worth having.
# cmd/quot/test/CMakeLists.txt says the same thing from the other end.
#
# The sources are generated at configure time into fsimg.d/ by CMakeLists.txt beside this
# file -- they are runs of one 64-byte line, so nothing binary is checked in -- and the
# paths below resolve against b6fsutil's WORKING_DIRECTORY, which is the test's build
# directory, exactly as ../../../root.manifest's resolve against kernel/test's.

default
owner 0
group 0
dirmode 0755
filemode 0644

dir /d

file /small
source fsimg.d/small

file /mid
owner 201
source fsimg.d/mid

file /big
owner 201
source fsimg.d/big

file /other
owner 202
source fsimg.d/other

file /d/nested
owner 202
source fsimg.d/nested
